Objective: This 21 day email course is designed for the student to gain
a clear understanding of the credit repair process so that they can
approach the client file and create an action plan with certainty. The
content of this course is also designed to help the student effectively
communicate credit information so that as a business owner they can feel
comfortable and confident when speaking about the subject of credit to
leads, clients, and the general public. Dispute 21 - Day 8:
5 Ways to Defeat Debt Collectors

NO. 3 - Debt Collector Practices & FDCPA Violations

Bruce, many credit repair companies will experience some form of
deletion success by chance. This is partly based on the way the dispute
resolution system is set up. Even though this is great, you don't want
to build your company and it's ability to get client results around
"chance." As the Repair Specialist, you should want to be as
knowledgeable about the When, What, Why and How of the dispute process.
As the Credit Expert, you will want to possess the knowledge to educate
your clients and identify ways in which their creditors and collectors
may be unlawfully attacking or violating their consumer rights. You will
also want to know what to do when this happens.  

Today's lesson is all about the foul practices of collection agencies
and debt collectors. Tomorrow we'll discuss how you should respond and
teach your clients to respond when their consumer rights are being
violated. By having an understanding of some of the ridiculous practices
used in their attempt to collect a debt and being able to recognize
where the violations lie, your confidence in dealing with debt
collectors will grow and your ability to properly educate the client as
well as use this as leverage to remove, negative, inaccurate,
unverifiable accounts from your clients credit report and possibly get
them some cash for their frustrations will have them referring you over
and over again!  

No.3 - Collection Agency Practices 

Study them. Understand them. Recognize them. Be Aware of Them. 

1) They threaten to have you arrested. Unfortunately for them, bad debts
are not a criminal matter. This type of threat is, however, a violation
of the FDCPA. 

2) They threaten to have your wages garnished. That cannot happen
without a judgment and a subsequent wage garnishing court procedure. In
some states wages cannot be garnished. That is another threat and
another FDCPA violation. 

3) They threaten to seize your bank account. Again, that cannot happen
without a judgment and a subsequent court order. All these threats are
in violation of the FDCPA and subject to $1000 awards each. 

4) If you have been making payments to a collection agency, your payment
records may get lost or the collector you were working with has left the
company. In any event, they will insist you still owe the original
amount, perhaps more. 

5) They add their own fees and interest to the original balance to
inflate the debt to get more money out of you. If the original contract
between the debtor and the original creditor does not allow for
collection fees and interest to the original balance then this is a
violation. In order for your to find out or for them to prove that what
they are doing is legal, they must present you with the original
contract, which they likely do not have.

6) They call your place of employment, your neighbors, or your relatives
to collect on the alleged debt, which are violations of the FDCPA. This
is why it is always important to include the cease and desist in your dv
letters to ensure communication is brought to a Holt until the debt has
been validated (More on this in #8). 

7) They give you a court summons instead of responding to your
validation letter. There is a court judgment precedent (below), which
says that a collection agency or attorney cannot file suit against you
if they haven't validated the debt within the initial 30- day period. If
this happens to you or your client, you may cite the case below. 

In Spears vs. Brennan the appeals court determined . . . 
"Brennan (plaintiff collection agency attorney) violated 15 U.S.C. §
1692g(b) [the Fair Debt Collection Practices Act] when he obtained a
default judgment against Spears (defendant) after Spears had notified
Brennan [within 30 days of receiving Brennan's debt collection notice]
in writing that the debt was being disputed and before Brennan had
mailed verification of the debt to Spears." The collection agency did
not validate the debt in response to Spear's DV letter, nor did the
collection attorney. This means that you have an absolute defense in
court to deny them judgment if they still have not validated the debt.
The collector cannot get a judgment until they respond to your request
for validation to comply with the FDCPA. 

8) In response to your debt validation letter, they ask for more
information about you on the phone or in writing. This is an attempt to
re-contract with you. A good debt validation letter can work if sent
Certified Mail Return Receipt within 30 days of receipt of the debt
collector's mini-Miranda notice. Under the FDCPA you can instruct the
collector to cease all collection activities and to not communicate with
you until they have validated the alleged debt, and then only to
communicate with you by mail, NO phone calls. If they do not do that,
they are in violation of the FDCPA and liable for $1000 each time they
communicate with you, as in asking for more information plus attorney
fees. Cha-Ching! 

9) Putting a negative listing on your credit report after you have
requested validation and before they have sent it is considered
collection activity and is a violation of the FDCPA. People have had
success sending a follow-up letterhead informing the collection agency
about that violation and instructing them to remove the listing. 

10) Forging Your Signature - Any person who's been in the CR business
long enough will advise you to not sign any written communications to
collection agencies. Signatures have been known to move from one
document to the next in an attempt to validate. This is why when you do
receive something from a debt collector that is supposed to resemble a
contract, many times it is without a signature. Do not let them fool you
and do not give them anything that will help their case.

11) Stealing your bank account information and debiting money from your
account, or just cleaning it out. If you and your client decides that it
will be best to settle a collection account, always, always, always send
the funds in the form of a money order or cashier's check. If you send a
personal check for any type of payment, the collection agency can take
your bank account number and routing information off of it and access
your account. Once they have your money it is up to you to get it back.
Of course they will claim you owe it and you authorized the debit with
your account information.

12) Lying - a popular expression in the industry has it that "100
percent of debt collectors lie 100 percent of the time." Individual
debt collectors tell lies to scare you into paying and complying. They
do that over the telephone, where there is not record of the lie. It is
difficult to lie in writing, when there is a documented record of
communications. This is why you should always take control of the
situation and get them to only communicate in writing. 

13) Encourage small, token payments to re-age a debt and restart the
applicable statute of limitations (SOL). There will be times when you
advise your clients not to pay a debt, but the hassle and phones calls
from debt collectors will frustrate them to the point of paying, even if
it is a small portion of the balance, just to stop the phone calls. It
is important that you simply communicate how the Statue of Limitations
works so that they are equipped to handle these situations until you can
contact the collectors on their behalf to cease communication. That is
what happens when you make a payment on a debt because it is a pain to
deal with even though it that has been discharged and is out of SOL.
This re-aging then opens you up to the possibility of a lawsuit over the
newly aged debt. The rationale goes since you are making payments, you
are probably worth suing. This is why "doing the right thing" is the
WRONG thing to do once a debt has been sold. 

14) Renaming to re-age a debt - A few junk debt buyers will attempt to
portray themselves as the original creditor of their junk debt purchases
to the credit reporting agencies. That way they can re-age the debt to
counter the SOL and get a negative listing onto your credit report.
Before disputing accounts, be sure to do your research and thoroughly
question the client about each account on the report so that you can
stop this trick in its track.

15) You send a collector a debt validation letter denying and disputing
the debt and asking for validation. They reply with forms to fill out so
you can claim your identity was stolen. This is a scam to avoid
validation. You or your client's identity was not stolen, and you should
not claim that it was. Send another deny/dispute/validate letter CMRRR
in response to those forms. 

16) Claiming over the telephone that a lawsuit has been filed against
you in your local court, and that the summons is on its way to you. This
is a great, scary lie. This phony lawsuit threat is usually done with
their first phone call to you before their mini-Miranda notice has
arrived telling you of your right to dispute the debt. This ambush phone
call out-of-the-blue from an unknown debt collector is a favorite,
effective collection tactic. It catches the consumer unprepared and
feeling guilty about the debt they know you owe. As you may have learned
in an earlier lesson, educate your client to get the name of the firm
and get off the phone without saying anything other than "I do not
discuss my personal finances over the phone with unknown strangers."
And, it is an FDCPA violation to threaten nonexistent lawsuits. 

17) They do not respond to debt validation letters. Or, instead of
responding to your DV, they bounce the debt to another collection
agency, that sends you another miniMiranda, and around and around. Just
know that if one CA can't validate neither can the other. Stop them dead
in their tracks by making this known to the new CA. 

18) Junk Debt Buyers and collection agencies review your credit reports
and put them through software that predicts the likelihood of your
ability to pay the debt. There is also soft used to inform the Debt
Buyers when you update your personal information with the credit bureaus
or even when your client is preparing to go through the home buying
process. Since the debt that Junk Debt Buyers purchase are often in the
form of spread sheets, many of them containing old and outdated
information (especially if the debt has been passed around and around),
when they attempt to contact the consumer they are many times
unsuccessful. So, they will let the debt lie dormant hoping at some
point they will get that "ding" in their system informing them of the
debtors new address, phone number, etc. so that they can contact them.
This also happens when applying for home loans and right before it's
time to close a new collection account pops up out of no where!  

Well there you have it. The most common collection practices to trick
consumers into paying unverifiable debt. 

Remember, Study them. Understand them. Recognize them. Be Aware of
Them.  

If you enjoyed today's lesson, Day 9 will be even better!

