lor: #0174df; font-size: inherit; font-weight: inherit; line-height: inheri=
t; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">There is a court judgment preceden=
t (below), which says that a collection agency or attorney cannot file suit=
 against you if they haven't validated the debt within the initial 30- day =
period. If this=0Ahappens to you or your client, you may cite the case belo=
w. </span></span><br class=3D""><br class=3D""><span style=3D"color: #0174d=
f; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: inherit; text-decoratio=
n: inherit;" class=3D"">In Spears vs. Brennan the appeals court determined =
. . . <br class=3D""></span><span style=3D"color: #000000; font-size: 17px;=
 font-weight: inherit; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit; font=
-style: italic;" class=3D"">"Brennan (plaintiff collection agency attorney)=
 violated 15 U.S.C. =C2=A7 1692g(b) [the Fair Debt Collection Practices Act=
] when he obtained a default judgment against Spears (defendant) after Spea=
rs had notified Brennan [within 30 days of receiving Brennan=E2=80=99s debt=
 collection notice] in writing that the debt was being disputed and before =
Brennan had mailed verification of the debt to Spears." The collection agen=
cy did not validate the debt in response to Spear=E2=80=99s DV letter, nor =
did the collection attorney. This means that you have=0Aan absolute defense=
 in court to deny them judgment if they still have not validated the debt. =
The collector cannot get a judgment until they respond to your request for =
validation to comply with the FDCPA.</span> <br class=3D""><br class=3D""><=
span style=3D"color: inherit; font-size: 17px; font-weight: inherit; line-h=
eight: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">8) In response to you=
r debt validation letter, they ask for more information about you on the ph=
one or in writing. <span style=3D"color: #0174df; font-size: inherit; font-=
weight: inherit; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"=
">This is an attempt to re-contract with you. A good debt validation letter=
 can work if sent Certified Mail Return Receipt within 30 days of receipt o=
f the debt collector=E2=80=99s mini-Miranda notice. Under the FDCPA you can=
 instruct the collector to cease all collection activities and to not commu=
nicate with you until they have validated the alleged debt, and then only t=
o=0Acommunicate with you by mail, NO phone calls. If they do not do that, t=
hey are in violation of the FDCPA and liable for $1000 each time they commu=
nicate with you, as in asking for more information plus attorney fees. Cha-=
Ching! </span></span><br class=3D""><br class=3D""><span style=3D"color: in=
herit; font-size: 17px; font-weight: inherit; line-height: inherit; text-de=
coration: inherit;" class=3D"">9) Putting a negative listing on your credit=
 report after you have requested validation and before they have sent it is=
 considered collection activity and is a violation of the FDCPA. <span styl=
e=3D"color: #0174df; font-size: inherit; font-weight: inherit; line-height:=
 inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">People have had success sen=
ding a follow-up letterhead informing the collection agency about that viol=
ation and instructing them to remove the listing. </span></span><br class=
=3D""><br class=3D""><span style=3D"color: inherit; font-size: 17px; font-w=
eight: inherit; line-height:=0Ainherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D=
"">10) Forging Your Signature =E2=80=93 <span style=3D"color: #0174df; font=
-size: inherit; font-weight: inherit; line-height: inherit; text-decoration=
: inherit;" class=3D"">Any person who's been in the CR business long enough=
 will advise you to not sign any written communications to collection agenc=
ies. Signatures have been known to move from one document to the next in an=
 attempt to validate. This is why when you do receive something from a debt=
 collector that is supposed to resemble a contract, many times it is withou=
t a signature. Do not let them fool you and do not give them anything that =
will help their case.</span></span><br class=3D""><br class=3D""><span styl=
e=3D"color: inherit; font-size: 17px; font-weight: inherit; line-height: in=
herit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">11) Stealing your bank account=
 information and debiting money from your account, or just cleaning it out.=
 <span style=3D"color: #0174df; font-size: inherit;=0Afont-weight: inherit;=
 line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">If you and you=
r client decides that it will be best to settle a collection account, alway=
s, always, always send the funds in the form of a money order or cashier's =
check. </span><span style=3D"color: #0174df; font-size: inherit; font-weigh=
t: inherit; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">If =
you send a personal check for any type of payment, the collection agency ca=
n take your bank account number and routing information off of it and acces=
s your account. Once they have your money it is up to you to get it back. O=
f course they will claim you owe it and you authorized the debit with your =
account information.</span></span><br class=3D""><br class=3D""><span style=
=3D"color: inherit; font-size: 17px; font-weight: inherit; line-height: inh=
erit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">12) Lying =E2=80=93 a popular e=
xpression in the industry has it that =E2=80=9C100 percent of debt collecto=
rs lie 100=0Apercent of the time.=E2=80=9D <span style=3D"color: #0174df; f=
ont-size: inherit; font-weight: inherit; line-height: inherit; text-decorat=
ion: inherit;" class=3D"">Individual debt collectors tell lies to scare you=
 into paying and complying. They do that over the telephone, where there is=
 not record of the lie. It is difficult to lie in writing, when there is a =
documented record of communications. This is why you should always take con=
trol of the situation and get them to only communicate in writing. </span><=
/span><br class=3D""><br class=3D""><span style=3D"color: inherit; font-siz=
e: 17px; font-weight: inherit; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inher=
it;" class=3D"">13) Encourage small, token payments to re-age a debt and re=
start the applicable statute of limitations (SOL). <span style=3D"color: #0=
174df; font-size: inherit; font-weight: inherit; line-height: inherit; text=
-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">There will be times when you advise your =
clients not to pay a debt, but the=0Ahassle and phones calls from debt coll=
ectors will frustrate them to the point of paying, even if it is a small po=
rtion of the balance, just to stop the phone calls. It is important that yo=
u simply communicate how the Statue of Limitations works so that they are e=
quipped to handle these situations until you can contact the collectors on =
their behalf to cease communication. That is what happens when you make a p=
ayment on a debt because it is a pain to deal with even though it that has =
been discharged and is out of SOL. This re-aging then opens you up to the p=
ossibility of a lawsuit over the newly aged debt. The rationale goes since =
you are making payments, you are probably worth suing. This is why =
=E2=80=9Cdoing the right thing=E2=80=9D is the WRONG thing to do once a deb=
t has been sold. </span></span><br class=3D""><br class=3D""><span style=3D=
"color: inherit; font-size: 17px; font-weight: inherit; line-height: inheri=
t; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">14) Renaming to re-age a debt=0A=
=E2=80=93 <span style=3D"color: #0174df; font-size: inherit; font-weight: i=
nherit; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">A few j=
unk debt buyers will attempt to portray themselves as the original creditor=
 of their junk debt purchases to the credit reporting agencies. That way th=
ey can re-age the debt to counter the SOL and get a negative listing onto y=
our credit report. Before disputing accounts, be sure to do your research a=
nd thoroughly question the client about each account on the report so that =
you can stop this trick in its track.</span></span><br class=3D""><br class=
=3D""><span style=3D"color: inherit; font-size: 17px; font-weight: inherit;=
 line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">15) You send a=
 collector a debt validation letter denying and disputing the debt and aski=
ng for validation. They reply with forms to fill out so you can claim your =
identity was stolen. <span style=3D"color: #0174df; font-size: inherit; fon=
t-weight: inherit;=0Aline-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=
=3D"">This is a <span style=3D"color: #ff0000; font-size: inherit; font-wei=
ght: bold; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">scam=
</span> to avoid validation. You or your client's identity was not stolen, =
and you should not claim that it was. Send another deny/dispute/validate le=
tter CMRRR in response to those forms. </span></span><br class=3D""><br cla=
ss=3D""><span style=3D"color: inherit; font-size: 17px; font-weight: inheri=
t; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">16) Claiming=
 over the telephone that a lawsuit has been filed against you in your local=
 court, and that the summons is on its way to you. <span style=3D"color: #0=
174df; font-size: inherit; font-weight: inherit; line-height: inherit; text=
-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">This is a great, scary lie. This phony la=
wsuit threat is usually done with their first phone call to you before thei=
r mini-Miranda notice has arrived telling=0Ayou of your right to dispute th=
e debt. This ambush phone call out-of-the-blue from an unknown debt collect=
or is a favorite, effective collection tactic. It catches the consumer unpr=
epared and feeling guilty about the debt they know you owe. As you may have=
 learned in an earlier lesson, educate your client to get the name of the f=
irm and get off the phone without saying anything other than =E2=80=9CI do =
not discuss my personal finances over the phone with unknown strangers.=
=E2=80=9D And,<span style=3D"color: inherit; font-size: inherit; font-weigh=
t: bold; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: underline;" class=3D""> it =
is an FDCPA violation</span> to threaten nonexistent lawsuits. </span></spa=
n><br class=3D""><br class=3D""><span style=3D"color: inherit; font-size: 1=
7px; font-weight: inherit; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;"=
 class=3D"">17) They do not respond to debt validation letters. Or, instead=
 of responding to your DV, they bounce the debt to another collection agenc=
y,=0Athat sends you another miniMiranda, and around and around. <span style=
=3D"color: #0174df; font-size: inherit; font-weight: inherit; line-height: =
inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">Just know that if one CA can=
't validate neither can the other. Stop them dead in their tracks by making=
 this known to the new CA. </span><br class=3D""><br class=3D"">18) Junk De=
bt Buyers and collection agencies review your credit reports and put them t=
hrough software that predicts the likelihood of your ability to pay the deb=
t. <span class=3D"" style=3D"color: #0174df; font-size: inherit; font-weigh=
t: inherit; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;">There is also =
soft used to inform the Debt Buyers when you update your personal informati=
on with the credit bureaus or even when your client is preparing to go thro=
ugh the home buying process. Since the debt that Junk Debt Buyers purchase =
are often in the form of spread sheets, many of them containing old and out=
dated information=0A(especially if the debt has been passed around and arou=
nd), when they attempt to contact the consumer they are many times unsucces=
sful. So, they will let the debt lie dormant hoping at some point they will=
 get that "ding" in their system informing them of the debtors new address,=
 phone number, etc. so that they can contact them. This also happens when a=
pplying for home loans and right before it's time to close a new collection=
 account pops up out of no where! </span> =C2=A0</span></div>=0A</div>=0A</=
